  "description": "Perform inner, left, or right database-style joins between two row-oriented datasets on a common key.\n\nMerging combines heterogeneous tables on shared keys, underpinning entity resolution and feature store aggregation pipelines.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\mathbf{D}_{\\text{out}} = \\text{df}_1 \\bowtie_{\\text{key}} \\text{df}_2\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Convert row records into pandas DataFrames\n2. Perform relational merge specifying join key attribute and join type ('inner', 'left', 'right')\n3. Convert resulting joined records back to list of dictionaries\n\n### Returns\nList of joined record dictionaries.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Merge DataFrames\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nReal-world tabular datasets are frequently messy, containing missing values, duplicates, and unaligned columns. Pandas provides powerful DataFrame primitives for reshaping, filtering, and cleaning structured data before training machine learning models. **Merge DataFrames** performs structured data manipulation efficiently.\n\nMerging combines heterogeneous tables on shared keys, underpinning entity resolution and feature store aggregation pipelines. In this problem, the goal is to: perform inner, left, or right database-style joins between two row-oriented datasets on a common key.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{D}_{\\text{out}} = \\text{df}_1 \\bowtie_{\\text{key}} \\text{df}_2\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Convert row records into pandas DataFrames\n2. **Phase 2**: Perform relational merge specifying join key attribute and join type ('inner', 'left', 'right')\n3. **Phase 3**: Convert resulting joined records back to list of dictionaries\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Vectorized Array Operations**: Applying vectorized transformations operates on contiguous C memory buffers under the hood, running orders of magnitude faster than iterating with Python loops.\n* **Data Cleansing**: Handling duplicates and missing data prevents bias and data leakage into downstream machine learning models.\n* **Memory Management**: Choosing appropriate column dtypes (e.g. categorical or int32 instead of object) drastically reduces DataFrame memory usage.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: left = [{\"user_id\": 1, \"name\": \"Alice\"}, {\"user_id\": 2, \"name\": \"Bob\"}], right = [{\"user_id\": 1, \"score\": 95}, {\"user_id\": 3, \"score\": 80}], on = \"user_id\", how = \"inner\"\n\nOutput: [{\"user_id\": 1, \"name\": \"Alice\", \"score\": 95}]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated pandas_merge_dataframes under standard test conditions."
